{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":61795657,"ns":0,"title":"Gennadiy Borisov","revisions":[{"revid":1377880902,"parentid":1364206621,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-10-01T18:38:01Z","size":14506,"sha1":"cc58b10bc11a2bae3fecc9eea484008bf45ac0bb","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{short description|Amateur astronomer who discovered the first known interstellar comet}}\n{{infobox person\n| image       = Gennady Borisov.jpg\n| birth_name  = Gennadiy Vladimirovich Borisov<!-- Source for \"Vladimirovich\": -->{{r|AstronomicalScientificCenter}}\n| birth_date  = {{birth year and age|1962}}{{r|RusskayaPlaneta}}\n| birth_place = [[Kramatorsk]], USSR{{r|RusskayaPlaneta}}\n| spouse      = \n| occupation  = {{ubl|[[amateur telescope making|Telescope maker]]|[[Amateur astronomer]]}}\n| employer    = [[Sternberg Astronomical Institute]]\n| known_for   = Discovery of [[2I/Borisov]]\n| awards      = multiple [[Edgar Wilson Award]]s\n| module      = {{infobox observatory\n| child           = yes\n| fetchwikidata   = NONE\n| name            = '''MARGO observatory'''\n| image           = \n| caption         = \n| code            = [[List of observatory codes#L51|L51]]\n| named_after     = \n| location        = [[Nauchnyi]], [[Bakhchysarai Raion]], [[Crimea]]\n| coordinates     = {{coord|44|43|35|N|34|0|45|E|display=inline}}\n| altitude        = {{cvt|574|m|ft}}\n| observing_time  = \n| established     = 2013{{r|MinorPlanetCirculars}}\n| website         = \n| telescope1_name = GENON\n| telescope1_type = {{val|0.192|u=m}} ({{f/|1.54}}){{r|AstronomicalScientificCenter}}\n| telescope2_name = GENON\n| telescope2_type = {{val|0.192|u=m}} ({{f/|1.54}})\n| telescope3_name = GENON Max\n| telescope3_type = {{val|0.300|u=m}} ({{f/|1.5}}){{r|AstronomicalScientificCenter}}\n| telescope4_name = GENON Max\n| telescope4_type = {{val|0.300|u=m}} ({{f/|1.5}})\n| telescope5_name = HGB-650\n| telescope5_type = {{val|0.650|u=m}} ({{f/|1.5}})\n}}\n}}\n'''Gennadiy Vladimirovich Borisov''' ({{langx|ru|Генна́дий Влади́мирович Бори́сов}}; born 1962 in [[Kramatorsk]]){{r|RusskayaPlaneta}} is a Crimean<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/07/science/interstellar-comet-2i-borisov.html |title=The Interstellar Comet Has Arrived in Time for the Holidays |last=Overbye |first=Dennis |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=7 December 2019 |access-date=25 March 2020}}</ref> [[amateur telescope making|telescope maker]] and [[amateur astronomy|amateur astronomer]] who discovered the first-known [[interstellar comet]], [[2I/Borisov]], in 2019.\n\n== Work ==\nBorisov works as an engineer at the {{ill|Crimean Astronomical Station|ru|Крымская лаборатория ГАИШ МГУ}}<ref group=Note>Not to be confused with the [[Crimean Astrophysical Observatory]], which is located right next to it.</ref> of the [[Sternberg Astronomical Institute]] of [[Moscow State University]].{{r|Sky&Telescope}}{{r|StateAstronomicalInstitute}} There, he maintains the telescopes, but does not make observations himself. He also works with Astronomicheskiy Nauchnyy Tsentr JSC, creating experimental telescopes in collaboration with [[Roscosmos]].{{r|Fontanka.ru}}\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"float: right; clear: right; margin-left: 1em;\"\n|+ Comets discovered by Gennadiy Borisov{{r|JPLSolarSystemDynamics}}\n! Designation !! Type\n|-\n| [[C/2013 N4]] (Borisov) || [[comet#Long period|long-period]]\n|-\n| C/2013 V2 (Borisov) || [[comet#Long period|hyperbolic]]\n|-\n| C/2014 R1 (Borisov) || [[comet#Long period|near-parabolic]]\n|-\n| [[C/2014 Q3]] (Borisov) || [[comet#Short period|Halley-type]]\n|-\n| C/2015 D4 (Borisov) || long-period\n|-\n| C/2016 R3 (Borisov) || long-period\n|-\n| C/2017 E1 (Borisov) || hyperbolic\n|-\n| [[2I/Borisov]] || hyperbolic, [[interstellar object|interstellar]]\n|-\n| C/2019 V1 (Borisov) || [[comet#Long period|near-parabolic]]\n|-\n|C/2020 Q1 (Borisov)\n|[[List of long-period comets|long-period]]\n|-\n| C/2021 L3 (Borisov) || hyperbolic\n|-\n| C/2023 T2 (Borisov) || near-parabolic\n|-\n| C/2024 V1 (Borisov) || long-period\n|-\n| C/2025 B2 (Borisov) || hyperbolic\n|-\n| C/2025 J1 (Borisov) || near-parabolic\n|-\n| C/2025 V1 (Borisov) || hyperbolic\n|}\n\nBorisov pursues astronomy in his spare time at his personal observatory MARGO located in [[Nauchnyi]], in the southern part of the Crimean peninsula. Between 2013 and 2019, he discovered nine comets{{r|JPLSolarSystemDynamics}} and several [[near-Earth object]]s (NEOs) such as {{mpl|2013 TV|135}}.{{r|Sky&Telescope}} These discoveries were made using telescopes he designed and built himself: GENON (2 comets), GENON Max (5 comets){{r|Fontanka.ru}}{{r|AstronomicalScientificCenter}} and the HGB-650 0.65&nbsp;m telescope (2 comets, including 2I/Borisov). In 2014, Borisov received two [[Edgar Wilson Award]]s for his discoveries of C/2013 N4 and C/2013 V2.{{r|CentralBureauForAstronomicalTelegrams}} Borisov also discovered the asteroid [[2023 BU]], another near-Earth object that passed within 4,000&nbsp;km of the Earth in January 2023. He also observed cometary activity on NEO [[(523822) 2012 DG61|(523822) 2012 DG<sub>61</sub>]], making it a Near-Earth Comet (NEC). The cometary activity was later independently discovered and confirmed by scientists of the [[Active Asteroids (citizen science project)|Active Asteroids]] project.<ref name=\"Chandler2025\"/>\n\n== Discovery of 2I/Borisov ==\nIn early 2019, Borisov completed his new 0.65-meter telescope.{{r|Gazeta.Ru}}{{r|Sky&Telescope}} On August 30, 2019, he used this telescope to discover the first known interstellar comet, 2I/Borisov, which is only the second interstellar object to have been observed.{{r|Sky&Telescope}}{{r|NASA}}\n\nBorisov described his discovery thus:{{r|RIANovosti}}\n\n{{cquote|I observed it on August 29, but it was August 30 [[GMT]]. I saw a moving object in the frame, it moved in a direction that was slightly different from that of [[main-belt|main asteroids]].<ref group=Note>Of the 800,000 objects known to orbit the Sun, [https://minorplanetcenter.net/mpc/summary 737,000] (92%) of them are [[main-belt]] asteroids.</ref> I measured its coordinates and consulted the [[Minor Planet Center]] database. Turned out, it was a new object. Then I measured the near-Earth object rating,<ref group=Note>[https://minorplanetcenter.net//iau/NEO/PossNEO.html NEO Rating] for calculating the probability that a new object is a near-Earth candidate.</ref> it is calculated from various parameters, and it turned out to be 100% – in other words, [[potentially hazardous object|dangerous]]. In such cases I must immediately post the parameters to the world webpage for confirmation of dangerous asteroids.<ref group=Note>[https://www.minorplanetcenter.net/iau/NEO/toconfirm_tabular.html The NEO Confirmation Page] for confirming near-Earth objects and potentially hazardous objects.</ref> I posted it and wrote that the object was diffuse and that it was not an asteroid, but a comet.}}\n\nThe discovery of 2I/Borisov by Gennadiy Borisov has been compared to the discovery of [[Pluto]] by [[Clyde Tombaugh]].{{r|Fontanka.ru}} Tombaugh was also an amateur astronomer who was building his own telescopes, although he discovered Pluto using [[Lowell Observatory]]'s [[astrograph]].\n\n== Discovery of 2023 BU ==\nOn January 21, 2023, Borisov discovered the relatively small asteroid [[2023 BU]], only 5 days before it would pass an initially calculated {{cvt|9975 ± 27|km|mi}}{{citation needed|date=January 2023}} from the centerpoint of Earth, closer than geostationary satellites but further than [[low Earth orbit]] satellites.\n\n== Views ==\nBorisov thinks that soon amateur astronomers will no longer be able to discover new comets: \"In 2016, only I discovered a comet. In 2013, there were seven of us. Every year there are less and less. There are more and more huge telescopes. Amateurs will soon have nothing left.\"{{r|Fontanka.ru}}\n\n==See also==\n* [[List of comets discov